g_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2", "B1.2"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=67%, source=both]\n\nDrug-Induced Hyperglycemia vs Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus with Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, anxiety, arrhythmia, blurry vision, coma\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant inheritance, blurred vision, dry mouth, extreme thirst, fever\n\nDrug-Induced Hyperglycemia vs Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus with Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, anxiety, arrhythmia, blurry vision, coma\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant inheritance, blurred vision, dry mouth, extreme thirst, fever\n\nDrug-Induced Hyperglycemia vs Secondary Diabetes Mellitus with Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, anxiety, arrhythmia, blurry vision, coma\n  Favours second: blurred vision, dry mouth, extreme thirst, fever, frequent urination\n\nType 2 Diabetes Mellitus with Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State vs Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus with Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State:\n  Favours first:  increased waist to hip ratio, insulin resistance, late onset, type ii diabetes mellitus\n  Favours second: type i diabetes mellitus\n\nType 2 Diabetes Mellitus with Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State vs Secondary Diabetes Mellitus with Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State:\n  Favours first:  autosomal dominant inheritance, increased waist to hip ratio, insulin resistance, late onset, type ii diabetes mellitus\n\nType 1 Diabetes Mellitus with Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State vs Secondary Diabetes Mellitus with Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State:\n  Favours first:  autosomal dominant inheritance, type i diabetes mellitus\n\nRelated to Drug-Induced Hyperglycemia: hemoglobinopathy, methemoglobinemia\n\nRelated to Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus with Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State: diabetes mellitus (disease), diabetes mellitus, noninsulin-dependent, diabetic ketoacidosis\n\nRelated to Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus with Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State: autoimmune disease of endocrine system, autoimmune polyendocrine, diabetes mellitus (disease)\n\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.75):]\n  Source: Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state: a historic review of the clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment. > History and Physical\n  If an infectious process precedes HHS, signs and symptoms include:\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.78):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  In a series of 48,005 inpatients over a 20-year period, morbilliform rash (91%) and urticaria (6%) were the most frequent skin reactions. Severe reactions are actually too rare to be detected in such \n[RAG context for 'and a painful rash' (score=0.73):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  In a series of 48,005 inpatients over a 20-year period, morbilliform rash (91%) and urticaria (6%) were the most frequent skin reactions. Severe reactions are actually too rare to be detected in such \n[RAG context for 'and a painful rash' (score=0.75):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  Although drug reactions have many manifestations, including urticaria, exanthematous drug-induced eruptions (Chap. 74) are most common and are often difficult to distinguish from viral exanthems. Erup\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  malaise → systemic inflammation → Infectious Erythematous Rash with Systemic Involvement\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Investigate markers of systemic inflammation (e.g., CRP, ESR) to support the diagnosis of an infectious erythematous rash\n  a painful rash → inflammatory skin reaction → Infectious Erythematous Rash with Systemic Involvement\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Perform a skin biopsy or culture to identify the cause of the painful rash and confirm an infectious etiology"}
